## The idea

Same as Permutations, but equal numbers would produce the same ordering many times. **Sort** a copy so equal numbers are next to each other, then use equal copies **only from left to right**: skip a number if the equal number just before it is not in use yet.

## Step by step

1. Sort a copy of the input.
2. Backtrack as in Permutations.
3. Skip index i when nums[i] == nums[i-1] and used[i-1] is false — starting with the second copy would repeat an ordering already built from the first.

## Worked example

**Input:** nums = [1, 1, 2] (sorted)

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| first position: index 0 (1) | → [1,1,2], [1,2,1] |
| first position: index 1 (1) | skip — index 0 is equal and unused |
| first position: index 2 (2) | → [2,1,1] |

**Result:** 3 distinct orderings instead of 6 with repeats

## Cost

O(n · n!) in the worst case (all distinct); much less with many repeats. O(n) extra memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 47 — every DISTINCT ordering when the numbers may repeat.
public static class UniquePermutations {
    public static IList<IList<int>> All(int[] nums) {
        var result = new List<IList<int>>();
        if (nums == null) return result;
        var sorted = (int[])nums.Clone();
        Array.Sort(sorted);                    // equal numbers sit next to each other
        Build(sorted, new bool[sorted.Length], new List<int>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private static void Build(int[] nums, bool[] used, List<int> current, List<IList<int>> result) {
        if (current.Count == nums.Length) { result.Add(new List<int>(current)); return; }
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            // The one new idea: among equal numbers, always use them left to right.
            // If the previous equal copy is NOT used at this point, picking this copy would
            // start the same ordering a second time — skip it.
            if (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1] && !used[i - 1]) continue;
            used[i] = true; current.Add(nums[i]);
            Build(nums, used, current, result);
            current.RemoveAt(current.Count - 1); used[i] = false;
        }
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Why `!used[i - 1]`?**

It forces equal copies to be placed in left-to-right order, so each distinct arrangement has exactly one way to be built.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n · n!) worst case (all distinct); far fewer with many repeats.

**Q: Alternative?**

Count each value in a map and recurse over distinct keys, decrementing counts — no sort, no skip rule.

**Q: Shared state?**

None.

**Q: Huge n with few distinct values.**

The count-map version is natural here: the branching factor is the number of distinct values.

**Q: How would you test it?**

Compare with "all permutations, de-duplicated with a set" on small random inputs.

